**Read:** [Termodinâmica dos Materiais — Cota, 2026](../sources/Termodinamica-dos-Materiais-Cota.lingua#r_05ES8F38ASNQEQMZ0HY6709Z0Z), chapter 11, especially surface energy, wetting, curvature and adsorption; [Materials kinetics — MIT 3.205 and 3.21](../sources/Kinetic-Processes-in-Materials-MIT.lingua#r_2BZVQ9NG9Y2XTF4QG0H3JYYS16), 3.21 lectures 5 and 17–20.

An interface contributes excess free energy, and its area, orientation, chemistry and strain matter. For an isotropic spherical particle with constant interfacial energy γ and molar volume V_m, a simple capillarity model shifts chemical potential by 2γV_m/r. Halving radius doubles this shift within that model.

For an ideal liquid on a smooth homogeneous rigid solid at equilibrium, Young's relation gives γ_SV−γ_SL=γ_LV cos θ. Hysteresis, roughness, reaction and pinning can prevent a measured contact angle from being that ideal equilibrium angle. Solid surface stress and surface free energy are not universally interchangeable.

**Work:** Derive the spherical chemical-potential shift by varying area with amount. Compare a faceted crystal and an isotropic sphere, explaining what anisotropy changes. Design a wetting comparison that separates equilibrium assumptions from an advancing/receding observation.

